<clickhouse>
    <!-- Turns off the QUERY profiler, which is the single largest remaining
         contributor to system.trace_log.

         Why this file exists separately from config.d/log-retention.xml: these
         are user-PROFILE settings, not server settings. ClickHouse only reads
         them from users.xml / users.d, so putting them next to the <trace_log>
         TTL in config.d would parse without error and do nothing. That silent
         no-op is the trap; hence the separate mount in docker-compose.yml.

         Measured composition of trace_log on mainnet 2026-08-03, over ~4 days:

           Real         22,062,374   59.2%   query profiler, 1s per thread
           Memory        7,745,639   20.8%   memory profiler (KEPT, see below)
           MemoryPeak    7,349,165   19.7%   memory profiler (KEPT)
           CPU              91,755    0.2%   query profiler, CPU clock

         The Real/CPU half is disabled below. It had never been read: across the
         entire query_log history the only statements touching system.trace_log
         were a TTL inspection and the TRUNCATE from the 2026-07-30 cleanup,
         plus the queries from this investigation. Nothing in the application
         or the runbooks consumes it. Sampling every thread every second also
         costs CPU in signal handling and stack unwinding, so this is a small
         throughput win as well as ~1.1 GiB.

         The MEMORY half is deliberately left ON. This container runs under a
         4 GiB mem_limit that it has already exceeded once mid-mutation (see
         config.d/log-retention.xml), and memory traces are precisely what
         explains an OOM after the fact. Trading ~750 MiB for that answer is
         the wrong trade, so memory_profiler_step is untouched at its 4 MiB
         default.

         To profile a slow query later, re-enable per session rather than
         reverting this file:
           SET query_profiler_real_time_period_ns = 1000000000;
         Setting either knob to 0 disables that sampler. -->
    <profiles>
        <default>
            <query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_real_time_period_ns>
            <query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>0</query_profiler_cpu_time_period_ns>
        </default>
    </profiles>
</clickhouse>
